### 2026-08-14T02:38:40.624Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Implemented per your design:

- `pasteClipboard` no longer calls any settling — cells land exactly where the raw canonical→gravity mapping puts them, mid-air or not (the "snow of 1x1s" approach; independent per-cell placement stays as-is, not made rigid).
- Settling is now `settleFloatingCellsStep()` — exactly one row per call — wired into `tick()` ahead of the active piece's own step. Since Gravity is paused whenever you're off in another mode pasting, nothing falls until you resume; once resumed, loose cells fall one tick at a time, audibly and visibly, exactly like a freshly-dropped piece, and normal play resumes once nothing's left floating.
- Also deduplicated the connected-component grouping: `dropRowsAbove` had its own inline copy of exactly what `_boardComponents()` does (a stale comment even said `_boardComponents` was "factored out" from it, but it never actually got switched over) — now both share one `_groupIntoComponents()`.

All Gravity/paste/INV-48 tests pass (57/57), plus a new test asserting the fall happens one row per tick rather than jumping straight to rest. Full suite is running in the background now as a final check.


