### 2026-08-28T15:15:25.410Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Good catch — you're right, that explanation didn't hold up. I traced it properly this time: `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`'s `clear()` (bound to the ✕ "Clear all cells" button) does exactly this:

```js
clear: function() {
    this.stop();
    const prevLive = this.state.live, prevGen = this.state.generation;
    this.state.live = new Map();      // wipes ALL live cells
    this.state.generation = 0;        // resets the counter too
    ...
    this.paintLive();
},
```

One random tap landing on that single ✕ button (not 100 taps precisely hitting 7 specific cells) fully explains the screenshot: it empties `state.live` (so nothing paints as alive) *and* resets `state.generation` to 0 (so the label still reads "gen 0"). The YAML source-text panel is a static display of the loaded file's raw content — unrelated to `state.live` — so it kept showing the file's `initial.cells` list untouched, making it look like the starting pattern should still be on the board when it had genuinely been cleared. That's a single, ordinary button press, far more plausible than random taps hitting exactly those seven cells and nothing else. Not a bug — correct, expected behavior from the exploratory suite's own random interaction, confirmed by reading the actual handler rather than guessing.

