Sure — here's what each one actually checks and why I'm confident neither is something this session broke.

**1. Gravity edge-reach flake** ([REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO], random matrix)

The check: for every mode/size/drawer-state combination, the Tonnetz board should reach within ~2 cell-diameters of at least 2 screen edges — the "board actually fills the space instead of floating in the middle with margin all around" invariant. This run it failed at:

> `[Android phone] gravity, drawer open, 420x676` — reached 0 edges, margins ~9-10 cell-diameters on all four sides.

This test draws its viewport sizes from a fresh random seed *every run* ([REDACTED_SECRET]), logged so any specific failure is reproducible, but the sizes themselves aren't stable run to run. I hit this exact failure class earlier in this session too — at completely different random sizes (481×710, 590×866, 574×923, 456×757, 630×611, 340×552) — before I'd touched anything Gravity-related. That pattern (same failure mode, different random coordinates, present before any of my edits) points to a genuine gap in Gravity's own board-fit algorithm for some range of aspect ratios, not a one-off fluke and not something my drawer/Sandbox/Life changes could plausibly cause — I never touched [REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO] or any Gravity-specific CSS this session. Worth fixing at some point, but it's a separate, pre-existing bug from what we've been working on.

**2. `INV-5: Render.highlightByMidi re-pulses visibly` flake** (Tablet Chrome)

The check: hitting the same MIDI pitch twice in quick succession should produce two distinguishable flashes, not one continuous highlight — meaning there must be at least one real paint frame where the highlight class is *off* between the two pulses. It failed with "removed immediately on the second call" — the timing-sensitive part (waiting for an actual `requestAnimationFrame` to land) came out differently than expected.

This one I checked more directly: I ran it against a prior commit via `git stash` (before my Life-mode CSS was even applied) and it failed there too, with the identical error. That rules out anything from this session — it's an existing rAF-timing sensitivity in the test itself, the kind that's prone to flaking under real system load (which this session had plenty of, given how many test runs I stacked up back to back).

Neither is in the diff of anything I touched, and both have direct evidence (same-class failures pre-dating my changes) rather than just "probably fine."


