### 2026-08-28T20:44:35.405Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Good, same pattern in both — drawer open (title + full mode-tab bar) stacked on top of [REDACTED_PERSON]'s own panel (also fully open), simultaneously eating most of a short viewport.

I'd lean away from a bespoke "fold up like the drawer" mechanism for [REDACTED_PERSON] specifically — you're right that it's extra state, extra CSS, extra tests for a corner case. Two simpler options, roughly in order of how much I'd like each:

**1. Extend the drawer's *existing* auto-collapse to also count a tap inside the mode's own panel as "a real interaction."** Right now `setupDrawerAutoCollapse` only treats a tap on the board or D-pad as "the player is playing, collapse the drawer to give them room." But tapping Play/Step/the file dropdown in [REDACTED_PERSON]'s panel (or a carousel piece in [REDACTED_PERSON]'s) is just as real an interaction, and reclaiming just the *drawer's* space (title + mode tabs) is often the bigger win by itself — it's the one thing that's identically oversized across every mode, not just [REDACTED_PERSON]. This is zero new UI concept, just adding `#sidebar` to the same allowlist that already exists. Doesn't touch [REDACTED_PERSON]'s own panel at all.

**2. Shrink the panel further at very short heights, purely in CSS.** Add one more clause to the existing `max-height: 220px` rule — something like `@media (max-height: 560px) { max-height: 150px }` — so a tight viewport gets a more aggressive cap (maybe just the buttons + a couple lines of YAML, more internal scrolling) instead of a new interaction mode. Cheapest possible fix, but doesn't address the drawer half of the problem at all — the two would still stack.

My instinct is **1**, since it's the more general fix (helps [REDACTED_PERSON] at tiny sizes too, not just [REDACTED_PERSON]) and reuses a mechanism you've already reviewed and approved rather than introducing a new one. I can also do both — they're not mutually exclusive and neither is expensive. Want me to go with 1, or 1+2?

Meanwhile, I'm still mid-investigation on the Gravity stale-board-fit bug (found the ResizeObserver that's *supposed* to self-correct this, tracing why it isn't) — will report back on that shortly.


