### 2026-08-28T20:53:28.852Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Not terrible — I think it's actually the cleaner fit, and the overhead is genuinely low. Here's why:

**What moves and what doesn't:** `#life-controls` currently bundles two different kinds of thing into one panel — the *controls* (file dropdown, filename, Play/Step/Reset/Clear/Save buttons) and the *reference material* (the "gen N" + YAML rule-description text, which you read once to understand the ruleset and then mostly ignore while watching it run). Your proposal keeps the controls in the always-visible panel and moves just the YAML/rules block into the drawer. That split maps exactly onto the distinction the drawer redesign already drew for the title/mode-tabs ("genuinely only interesting once") versus the action-icon toolbar ("useful all the time") — Life's rules text is squarely in the first bucket.

**Concretely, what it costs:**
- One DOM element moves from `#life-controls` into `#top-drawer`, shown/hidden by the *exact* per-mode pattern `setMode` already uses everywhere else — not a new mechanism, one more line in code that already exists.
- `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`'s existing code that writes the rule display just targets a different element id. Same update logic, same triggers (load a file, step a generation), nothing new to keep in sync.
- No new CSS mechanism — the drawer's own collapse/scroll already handles arbitrary content length, which actually works out *better* for long rule descriptions than the panel's flat 220px cap does today.
- Bonus: there's a dead, unused `#drawer-injected-tools` div already sitting in `#top-drawer` from some earlier iteration (zero references anywhere in JS) — I'd either repurpose it or remove it and add a properly-named one; either way it's a wash, not added mess.

**Is "Life's the only mode using this" a smell?** I don't think so — Melody/Compose are already the only modes using `#notation-bar`, and nobody's treated that as overhead worth avoiding. The mechanism (drawer collapse) stays fully general; only the *content* differs per mode, same as it already does everywhere else in the sidebar.

One real design choice to make: portrait's collapsed drawer height is currently a fixed `60px` (fine for just title+tabs) — Life's rules text would need either its own taller cap or to just scroll within the drawer once expanded. I'd default to letting it scroll (matches how `#mode-controls` already scrolls in landscape), not a new fixed height.

Want me to build this?


